14:["$","li","b5101",{"children":[["$","strong","b5099",{"children":["Brief the whole job, not the next step. "]}],"Asking for the references, the variations, every ratio and a brand check in one instruction costs the same as asking for one of them."]}]
15:["$","li","b5104",{"children":[["$","strong","b5102",{"children":["Ambiguity gets a question, not a guess. "]}],"In ask-permission mode the agent checks aspect ratio, audience and tone before it spends a generation. In auto-run it decides and builds while you're elsewhere."]}]
16:["$","h2","b5106",{"id":"static-ads-that-match-a-look-you-already-have","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["Static ads that match a look you already have"]}]
17:["$","p","b5108",{"children":["To build a static ad from a style reference on Melius, you brief the Mel agent: point it at your reference images and ask for new statics in that style. It assembles the canvas, runs the style analysis, chooses the models, generates the variations, and leaves every step visible so you can correct the ones that missed. From brief to assets you can download runs about 10 to 15 minutes."]}]
18:["$","p","b5110",{"children":["Most of the work in matching a look goes into describing it. You know the campaign you want to sit next to, and you can see exactly why it works, but turning that into instructions a model will follow is the step that eats an afternoon. That's the step you're handing over."]}]
19:["$","h2","b5112",{"id":"what-you-actually-type","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["What you actually type"]}]
1a:["$","p","b5114",{"children":["Right-click anywhere on the canvas and choose Ask Mel, or press the forward slash key. Anything you have selected goes in as a reference automatically. Then describe the job. You don't need to name the model, set the resolution, or say which output connects to which input. Mel will do these for you."]}]
1b:["$","p","b5119",{"children":["Our standing advice is to ask for more than you think will work. A brief naming the references, the number of variations, every ratio you ship and a brand check against your brand anchor costs no more to write than one asking for a single image, and if the agent can't cover the whole job it tells you. Brief it the way you'd brief a designer, with a concrete goal and real constraints, not the way you'd chat. This is the same ",["$","$L3c","b5117",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5116","href":"/blog/brief-to-execution-workflow","isNewTab":false},"children":["brief-to-execution workflow"]}]," applied to a single look."]}]
1c:["$","h2","b5121",{"id":"what-the-agent-does-in-the-background","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["What the agent does in the background"]}]
1d:["$","p","b5123",{"children":["None of this needs doing by hand, and all of it stays inspectable while it happens. A cyan marker on the canvas shows where the agent is currently building."]}]
1e:["$","ul","b5126-parent",{"children":[["$","li","b5126",{"children":[["$","strong","b5124",{"children":["Reads your brand anchor. "]}],"If you keep one, the node holding your voice, palette and type gets pulled in as context before anything generates."]}],["$","li","b5129",{"children":[["$","strong","b5127",{"children":["Groups the references. "]}],"Your reference images become a single unified group, so the analysis reaches all of them on one edge instead of one per image."]}],["$","li","b5132",{"children":[["$","strong","b5130",{"children":["Runs the style analysis. "]}],"The Image Style Analysis template comes preconfigured but has inputs that need wiring, which is exactly the case to hand to the agent. It returns a written description: palette, direction and quality of the light, mood, composition, depth of field, shot type."]}],["$","li","b5138",{"children":[["$","strong","b5133",{"children":["Picks the model per step. "]}],"Melius recommends ",["$","$L3c","b5136",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5135","href":"/blog/nano-banana-image-models","isNewTab":false},"children":["Nano Banana Pro"]}]," for hewing to a photographic look, and GPT Image 2 where the ad carries visible copy, its High tier for production output. You can override that on any node."]}],["$","li","b5141",{"children":[["$","strong","b5139",{"children":["Writes the prompt. "]}],"Because the style description carries the look, the generation prompt only has to name the subject and where it sits. Short prompts win here, and the agent knows it."]}],["$","li","b5144",{"children":[["$","strong","b5142",{"children":["Builds the image nodes and connects them. "]}],"Style description into the image node, resolution set, ratio set per placement. The node hides any ratio the chosen model can't produce."]}],["$","li","b5147",{"children":[["$","strong","b5145",{"children":["Runs the variations in parallel. "]}],"Image models are probabilistic, so several takes at once beats one take and a retry. Ask for every ratio in the same brief and it queues those together too."]}]]}]
1f:["$","h2","b5149",{"id":"where-you-step-in","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["Where you step in"]}]
20:["$","p","b5151",{"children":["The first pass gets you most of the way, not all of it. We put that at 85% to 95%, and the last stretch is the part worth your attention, because it's taste rather than assembly."]}]
21:["$","p","b5154",{"children":[["$","strong","b5152",{"children":["Work at the node, not in the chat. "]}],"Open the one image that nearly landed, edit its prompt with the specific correction, and re-run that node alone. Product too small in frame becomes a line saying the product fills 40% of the frame width. Colour drifting green becomes a warmer grade with terracotta in the highlights. Illegible type on a label means switching that node to GPT Image 2 High."]}]
22:["$","p","b5156",{"children":["Go back to the agent for the genuinely bulk things: swapping a model across multiple nodes, regenerating everything with the brand anchor attached, resizing ten nodes into three ratios each."]}]
23:["$","p","b5158",{"children":["One habit worth forming. The agent doesn't automatically know about an edit you made by hand, so if you later ask it to repeat that treatment across the canvas it may revert to its original approach. Either make the change on a shared context node like the brand anchor, where it propagates on the next run, or tell the agent what you changed and ask it to apply that too."]}]
24:["$","h2","b5160",{"id":"where-this-workflow-goes-wrong","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["Where this workflow goes wrong"]}]
25:["$","ul","b5163-parent",{"children":[["$","li","b5163",{"children":[["$","strong","b5161",{"children":["The brief was too small. "]}],"Describing one step, running it, then describing the next is the safe instinct and it costs you the afternoon. A re-brief is cheap; orchestrating by hand is not."]}],["$","li","b5166",{"children":[["$","strong","b5164",{"children":["The references disagree with each other. "]}],"Five fashion editorials and one storefront screenshot do not share a look, and the description will average them into something you didn't ask for. The agent can only describe what your set has in common."]}],["$","li","b5169",{"children":[["$","strong","b5167",{"children":["The agent got treated like a chatbot. "]}],"It isn't a chat window, it builds canvases. Conversational back-and-forth gets far less out of it than one concrete brief carrying references and constraints."]}],["$","li","b5172",{"children":[["$","strong","b5170",{"children":["One node got edited fifteen times. "]}],"Past about the fifth tweak the problem usually isn't the prompt. Step back and re-brief, because the agent has the whole canvas as context and will often unstick it."]}],["$","li","b5175",{"children":[["$","strong","b5173",{"children":["The output was judged on the canvas preview. "]}],"Previews are downscaled at every resolution to keep the canvas quick. Open a variation full screen, or download it, before you rule it out."]}]]}]
26:["$","h2","b5177",{"id":"why-the-canvas-is-still-there","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["Why the canvas is still there"]}]
27:["$","p","b5179",{"children":["Melius is the Agentic OS for creative work, and underneath the agent is a node-based canvas where every model call is a node and edges pass one node's output into the next. The canvas isn't something you have to learn before you can work. It's there so the agent's decisions are legible: which model ran, what prompt it wrote, which reference fed which step."]}]
28:["$","p","b5181",{"children":["That's what separates this from describing what you want in a chat and hoping. Nothing here is a black box. When an output is wrong you find the node where it went wrong, change that one thing, and re-run everything downstream of it. Earlier node canvases were built before agents existed and assumed a human would wire up every node; this one exists so you can see and steer the work instead of performing it."]}]
29:["$","p","b5186",{"children":["The models sit behind ",["$","$L3c","b5184",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5183","href":"/blog/one-subscription-every-model","isNewTab":false},"children":["one subscription"]}],". Melius spans a library of leading models for image, video and audio, and the agent routes each step to the right one unless you choose per node."]}]
2a:["$","p","b5188",{"children":["You own the content you create on Melius and can use, publish, and distribute it commercially, subject to the underlying model providers' terms. Melius doesn't claim ownership of your work and doesn't use it to train AI models."]}]
2b:["$","p","b5193",{"children":["Have a look you want to hit? ",["$","$L3c","b5191",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5190","href":"https://app.melius.com","isNewTab":true},"children":["Open Melius"]}],", drop your references on a canvas, and tell the agent what you want."]}]
2c:["$","p","b5198",{"children":["For the next step, see ",["$","$L3c","b5196",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5195","href":"/blog/text-to-speech-voiceover","isNewTab":false},"children":["turning a script into a natural voiceover"]}],"."]}]
2d:["$","h2","b5200",{"id":"frequently-asked-questions","className":"scroll-mt-28","children":["Frequently asked questions"]}]
2e:["$","div","b5201",{"className":"faq-list my-10 flex flex-col divide-y divide-black/10 border-y border-black/10","children":[["$","details","0",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["Do I have to use the canvas to make static ads on Melius?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"No. You can brief the agent and steer the results, and plenty of people work that way. The canvas is where the agent's work becomes visible and editable, so you'll open it to fix the outputs that missed, but you don't wire the workflow yourself unless you want to."}]]}],["$","details","1",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["What should I put in the brief?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"The goal, your references, and the constraints that matter: how many variations, which ratios you ship, and the brand anchor to check against. Our example for this workflow is a single sentence asking it to use the Image Style Analysis template with your reference images and return a style description for new variants. Brief the whole job at once rather than one step at a time."}]]}],["$","details","2",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["Will the agent generate before checking with me?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"That depends on the mode. In ask-permission mode it raises clarifying questions before each major step, which suits high-stakes work where you want to see every decision. In auto-run it decides and builds while you're elsewhere, so a brief sent between meetings has a canvas of results waiting when you get back."}]]}],["$","details","3",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["How many reference images should I give it?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":"$L3d"}]]}],"$L3e"]}],"$L3f","$L40","$L41"]}]
2f:["$","p","b5206",{"children":["For product photography, follow our ",["$","$L3c","b5204",{"link":{"_type":"link","_key":"b5203","href":"/blog/product-hero-shot","isNewTab":false},"children":["product hero shot workflow"]}]," to keep specifications, branding, and style references separate."]}]
30:["$","section","related",{"className":"relative w-full pb-section bg-gray-background","children":[["$","$L11",null,{"color":"gray"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"relative z-10 mx-auto w-full max-w-300 px-5 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"t-h-2 mb-12 text-center text-balance text-gray-dark-1","children":"Related Articles"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto grid w-full max-w-max grid-cols-1 gap-11 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3","children":[["$","$L42","post-brief-to-execution-workflow",{"post":{"_key":"post-brief-to-execution-workflow","slug":"brief-to-execution-workflow","category":"modus-news","title":"The Brief-to-Execution workflow for creative teams","isoDate":"2026-07-16","date":"July 16, 2026","readTime":"7 Min Read","excerpt":"One-shot prompting has a ceiling. The agencies that win aren't the ones with the best prompt. They're the ones that turn a single master brief into a full, on-brand campaign. Here is the Brief-to-Execution workflow, and how an orchestration layer like Melius splits the Labor of Execution from the Labor of Taste.","image":{"url":"/images/blog/brief-to-execution/cover.webp"},"authors":[{"name":"Alex Chen, Member of Technical Staff"}],"faq":[{"question":"What is the Brief-to-Execution workflow?","answer":"Brief-to-Execution is a structured way of running creative production where a single master brief becomes the source of truth for every asset. Instead of prompting AI models one by one, a human defines the strategy, an orchestration layer like Melius turns that intent into work on a shared canvas, routing each job to the right model and holding every output to one brand context, and the human returns to select and polish the best results. It moves the work from fragmented chats into one unified production line."},{"question":"How does the Brief-to-Execution framework solve inconsistency in AI creative work?","answer":"By establishing a singular master brief as the source of truth, the framework ensures every output, whether copy, imagery, or video, derives from the same strategic intent rather than isolated prompts. This eliminates the drift that occurs when designers and writers prompt models independently, so a social post and a video script stay on-brand because they draw on the same brief and the same brand context."},{"question":"What is the difference between the Labor of Execution and the Labor of Taste?","answer":"The Labor of Execution is the repetitive, high-volume work: generating copy variations, resizing a master visual for fifteen aspect ratios, keeping every asset against your brand context. Agents handle it. The Labor of Taste is judgment: setting the creative vision, defining the never-go zones, and picking the one concept with the spark. Humans keep it."},{"question":"What does an orchestration layer like Melius actually do?","answer":"You store your brand context with Melius once, written instructions plus reference images, and Mel, its creative-director agent, applies it to every prompt, node title, and output automatically. Mel plans the work on a shared canvas and routes each job to the best model for it, so you steer in plain language instead of rewriting prompts one at a time."},{"question":"Why isn't one-shot prompting enough for agencies?","answer":"One-shot prompting forces a human to act as a manual operator, micromanaging each output in isolation. It doesn't scale, and it produces drift because every prompt is disconnected from the last. Agencies that need volume without losing brand consistency have to move from operating tools to architecting systems."}],"tableOfContents":[{"_key":"why-individual-models-aren-t-enough","title":"The prompting ceiling"},{"_key":"defining-the-brief-to-execution-framework","title":"The framework"},{"_key":"the-human-as-creative-director","title":"The creative director"},{"_key":"orchestrating-with-melius","title":"Orchestration"},{"_key":"future-proofing-the-agency-workflow","title":"Future-proofing"},{"_key":"frequently-asked-questions","title":"FAQ"}],"content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b407","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b404","text":"The competitive advantage in creative work is shifting. It is moving away from the person who can coax the best single output from a model, and toward the team that can turn a strategy into a full campaign without losing its brand soul along the way. That shift has a shape, and it is worth naming: the ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b405","text":"Brief-to-Execution","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b406","text":" workflow.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b409","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b408","text":"Why individual models aren't enough","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"why-individual-models-aren-t-enough"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b411","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b410","text":"The early days of generative AI were defined by the prompt. Creative professionals spent hours refining the perfect string of words to get a single image or a halfway-decent paragraph of copy. That era, the era of the prompt engineer, is hitting a ceiling. In a professional agency setting, one-shot prompting is an inefficient bottleneck. It requires the human to act as a manual operator, micromanaging every single output in isolation.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b417","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b412","text":"The real challenge for agencies today isn't ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b413","text":"how do I talk to a chatbot?","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b414","text":" but ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b415","text":"how do I scale creative production without losing brand soul?","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b416","text":" When you are prompting models one by one, you aren't being creative. You're being a data-entry clerk for an LLM. To move past this, we have to transition from being tool-operators to becoming system-architects.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b419","style":"h3","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b418","text":"The system-architect paradigm","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b424","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b420","text":"The system-architect paradigm is the transition from manually manipulating individual model inputs to designing automated, interconnected workflows that carry a high-level goal from brief to finished assets. Instead of asking ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b421","text":"what is the best prompt for this headline?","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b422","text":", the system-architect asks ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b423","text":"how do I build a pipeline that takes a 10-page brand strategy and turns it into 50 unique, on-brand assets?","marks":["em"]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b426","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b425","text":"Defining the Brief-to-Execution framework","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"defining-the-brief-to-execution-framework"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b428","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b427","text":"The solution to prompting fatigue is a structured Brief-to-Execution framework. This methodology moves the generative AI workflow away from fragmented chats and into a unified production line, where agents handle the volume and humans handle the vision.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b430","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b429","text":"By establishing a singular master brief as the source of truth, the framework ensures that every output, whether copy, visual assets, or video, derives from the same strategic intent rather than isolated, manual prompts. This shifts the human role from manual execution to orchestration, allowing for a cohesive multi-channel campaign where every output, from a social post to a video script, maintains brand alignment because it draws on the same brief and the same brand context. This centralized approach eliminates the drift that occurs when individual designers or writers prompt AI models independently, keeping the final execution close to the original strategy.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b432","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b431","text":"The framework consists of three distinct phases.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b435","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b433","text":"Strategic intent (human). ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b434","text":"The human defines the why and the who. This is the master brief containing brand voice, audience personas, and campaign goals.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b438","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b436","text":"Agent orchestration (Melius). ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b437","text":"The brief is ingested into an orchestration layer like Melius, which breaks the high-level intent into actionable tasks and builds them out on a shared canvas.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b441","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b439","text":"Refined output (human). ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b440","text":"The agents produce the Labor of Execution, and the human returns to perform the final Labor of Taste, selecting and polishing the best versions.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b443","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b442","text":"The human as creative director","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"the-human-as-creative-director"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b449","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b444","text":"In this new workflow, the human role evolves into that of a creative director. A creative director doesn't necessarily pick up the brush; they set the constraints and judge the results. This shift is critical because it separates the ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b445","text":"Labor of Execution","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b446","text":" from the ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b447","text":"Labor of Taste","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b448","text":".","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b451","style":"h3","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b450","text":"The Labor of Execution, assigned to agents","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b453","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b452","text":"Agents are far superior to humans at the repetitive, high-volume tasks that traditionally eat up agency hours.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b456","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b454","text":"Variations. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b455","text":"Creating 20 different versions of a headline for A/B testing.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b459","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b457","text":"Formatting. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b458","text":"Resizing a master visual for 15 different social-media aspect ratios.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b465","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b462","href":"/blog/static-ad-from-style-reference","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b460","text":"Contextualization. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b461","text":"Taking one campaign concept and building out the full set (the social cut, the email header, the video storyboard) all drawn from the same brief, or ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b463","text":"building a static ad from a style reference","marks":["b462"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b464","text":" so a new subject matches a look you already have.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b468","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b466","text":"On-brand output. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b467","text":"Holding every asset to the same stored brand context (voice, palette, typography, reference imagery) instead of eyeballing a 50-page guidelines deck by hand.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b470","style":"h3","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b469","text":"The Labor of Taste, retained by humans","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b472","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b471","text":"This is where the value of the human creative lies. Agents cannot feel the vibe of a cultural moment or understand the nuance of irony and empathy.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b475","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b473","text":"Setting the creative vision. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b474","text":"Deciding that a brand needs to feel nostalgic but futuristic rather than just modern.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b478","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b476","text":"Defining constraints. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b477","text":"Setting the ethical boundaries and the never-go zones for the AI.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b481","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b479","text":"Final quality gatekeeping. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b480","text":"The no or the yes. Looking at the 10 concepts generated by the agents and identifying the one that has the spark necessary for a successful campaign.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b483","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b482","text":"Orchestrating with Melius","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"orchestrating-with-melius"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b485","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b484","text":"Melius serves as the bridge between your strategic intent and the actual production of assets. It is the orchestration layer that prevents your workflow from devolving into a dozen browser tabs and disconnected conversations. When you use Melius, you aren't just sending a prompt to an AI; you are deploying a system-wide context.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b488","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b486","text":"Brand context, stored once. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b487","text":"You give Melius your brand context, written instructions plus reference images, and Mel applies it to every prompt, node title, and output automatically, so the work starts on-brand instead of you restating the rules each time.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b494","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b491","href":"/blog/one-subscription-every-model","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b489","text":"The right model for each job. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b490","text":"A campaign spans copy, images, video, and audio, and no single model is best at all of them. Mel plans the work and routes each job to the best model across ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b492","text":"every major provider under one subscription","marks":["b491"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b493","text":", then lays the results out together on one canvas.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b497","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b495","text":"One shared context. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b496","text":"Because every generation draws on the same brief and the same brand context on one canvas, the pieces stay coherent. If the brief calls for a dark, cinematic tone, the copy Mel writes and the images it generates pull from that same intent instead of drifting apart across separate chats.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b500","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b498","text":"Steer in plain language. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b499","text":"Instead of rewriting prompts one at a time, you tell Mel what to change (make it more aggressive, warmer grade, tighter crop) and it reworks the affected pieces on the canvas for you.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b502","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b501","text":"This level of orchestration is what allows a single creative director to manage the output of what would traditionally be a 10-person production team.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b504","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b503","text":"Future-proofing the agency workflow","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"future-proofing-the-agency-workflow"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b506","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b505","text":"The competitive advantage in the next five years will not belong to the agency that uses AI; it will belong to the agency that masters the Brief-to-Execution workflow. The benefits are measurable.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b509","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b507","text":"Speed. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b508","text":"Moving from brief to a full-funnel campaign in hours instead of weeks.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b512","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b510","text":"Consistency. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b511","text":"Holding one brand context across thousands of assets, so the same intent shows up whether it's the first render or the ten-thousandth.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b515","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b513","text":"Scale. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b514","text":"Handling 5x the client volume without a 5x increase in headcount or overhead.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b517","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b516","text":"We are moving away from the magic-box view of AI, where you type a prompt and hope for a miracle. We are entering the era of the agentic workforce, where tools like Melius let you stop being an operator and start being a director. Your value is no longer in your ability to use the tools; it's in your ability to lead the system.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b522","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b519","href":"https://app.melius.com","isNewTab":true}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b518","text":"Move beyond the prompt. ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b520","text":"Start directing your workflow with Melius","marks":["b519"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b521","text":".","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b524","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b523","text":"Frequently asked questions","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"frequently-asked-questions"},{"_type":"faqList","_key":"b525","items":"$30:props:children:1:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:post:faq"}]}}],"$L43","$L44"]}]]}]]}]
33:null
3b:"$d:props:children:1:props:serverProvidedParams:params"
7:300
3d:["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]
3e:["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"Two to four is usually enough, and the recipe works to a range of two to ten. That ceiling is editorial, not technical: individual models accept many more. More references do not produce a more accurate style description, because the analysis describes what your references have in common, so a sixth image sharing less with the others widens the description instead of sharpening it."}]
3f:["$","details","4",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["Can I use a competitor's ad as a style reference?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"Yes, and it's one of the most common inputs for this workflow, alongside your own past campaigns and fashion editorials. The analysis returns a description of photographic qualities such as light, palette and composition rather than a copy of the image, and you're generating your own subject against that description."}]]}]
40:["$","details","5",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["Can I reuse a style that worked?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"Yes. The style description is a text node, so it copies and pastes between canvases and you can point a later brief at it. Once one reliably produces on-brand output it becomes a small reusable asset you start every campaign canvas from."}]]}]
41:["$","details","6",{"className":"faq-item","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-gray-dark-1 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 font-medium select-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden","children":["How much does a batch of static ads cost to run?",["$","svg",null,{"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","aria-hidden":"true","className":"text-foreground-muted size-5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 [[open]_&]:rotate-180","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M6 9l6 6 6-6","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round"}]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"t-b-1 text-foreground-muted m-0 pr-8 pb-5","children":"Generating on Melius runs on credits, drawn from the same pool whichever model a step uses, so comparing two models on the same shot costs no more to set up than running one. Current plans and credit allowances are on the pricing page."}]]}]
43:["$","$L42","post-seedance-2-5-anticipation",{"post":{"_key":"post-seedance-2-5-anticipation","slug":"seedance-2-5-anticipation","category":"modus-news","title":"Seedance 2.5 is coming: ByteDance's next-gen AI video model","isoDate":"2026-07-07","date":"July 7, 2026","readTime":"6 Min Read","excerpt":"Seedance is behind more than a quarter of every video generation on Melius, and its share keeps climbing. ByteDance's Seedance 2.5 is announced with 50 reference inputs, synced audio, and, per its makers, native 4K. Here is the real usage data behind Seedance today, and an honest read on what 2.5 does and does not confirm.","image":{"url":"/images/blog/seedance-2-5/cover.webp"},"authors":[{"name":"Alex Chen, Member of Technical Staff"}],"faq":[{"question":"What is Seedance 2.5?","answer":"Seedance 2.5 is ByteDance's next-generation AI video model, announced on June 23, 2026. Per ByteDance, it generates clips up to 30 seconds long, accepts up to 50 multimodal reference inputs, and co-processes audio in the same latent space as the video so sound and action stay in sync. It has not been publicly released, so these are announced specifications rather than independently tested numbers."},{"question":"When is Seedance 2.5 coming out?","answer":"It is in global enterprise beta now, with public access rolling out in early July 2026. We route across every major model at Melius, so we will add Seedance 2.5 as soon as public access opens."},{"question":"What is new in Seedance 2.5 compared to Seedance 2.0?","answer":"The clearest change is reference capacity, up from 12 inputs to 50, covering images, audio, 3D white models, and style references. ByteDance also describes native audio synchronisation, local region editing, longer clips, and around 20% better prompt adherence. Native 4K with 10-bit color is reported too, though the 4K upgrade shown at the same event was attributed mainly to a Seedance 2.0 tier, and ByteDance has not published a separate resolution spec for 2.5, so treat 4K as an announced feature rather than a confirmed one."},{"question":"How much is Seedance used on Melius?","answer":"A lot. Seedance makes up 26.7% of all video generations on Melius, one of the two most-used video models on the platform, and about 4,400 teams have run it. Its share has climbed from 6.5% in March 2026 to nearly 30% today."},{"question":"How do you use Seedance 2.0?","answer":"You can use Seedance 2.0 on Melius today. Pick Seedance in the video model picker, give it a text prompt or, for production work, a reference image to animate, and run it. Because every model shares one credit pool, you can compare the same shot across Seedance, Kling and Veo side by side without juggling separate budgets."},{"question":"Is Seedance free to use?","answer":"Seedance runs on paid generation credits like every frontier video model. On Melius those credits are shared across every image, video and audio model in one pool, so you are not buying a separate Seedance subscription to try it."},{"question":"Will Seedance 2.5 be available on Melius?","answer":"It already is. Seedance 2.5 shipped and is in the Melius video model picker alongside Kling, Veo, Sora, and Wan, with 30-second clips, generated audio, and up to 50 reference files."}],"tableOfContents":[{"_key":"see-it-for-yourself","title":"See It"},{"_key":"a-quarter-of-every-video-and-climbing","title":"The Numbers"},{"_key":"what-ai-video-generation-models-struggle-at","title":"The Hard Part"},{"_key":"get-ready-for-it","title":"Get Ready"},{"_key":"frequently-asked-questions","title":"FAQ"}],"content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b157","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b154","text":"Right now, over a quarter of video generation traffic on Melius is routed to Seedance 2.0. Initially, Seedance wasn't heavily used, and didn't even cover a tenth of our generations in regards to videos. It just quietly became one of the models people here reach for when they need a quality video to post, and its share has climbed nearly every month since our inception in spring. When ByteDance announced ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b155","text":"Seedance 2.5","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b156","text":", it was not one more model to go and evaluate. It's an upgrade to something a lot of users are already excited about and are still pushing the boundaries of.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b159","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b158","text":"This piece is two things: what our own generation data says about how people use Seedance right now, and a read on what 2.5 promises. The usage numbers here are ours, and they are pulled directly from our data. Seedance 2.5 is not public yet, so its specs are based on what info ByteDance has released on it.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b161","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b160","text":"See it for yourself","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"see-it-for-yourself"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b163","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b162","text":"Start with the output, because that is what earns the usage. These are real Seedance 2.0 generations from Melius, one model across four different jobs: a product beauty shot, a character moment, a cinematic frame, and a scene with a lot of motion. Not a cherry-picked hero reel, just the kind of work people run through it every day.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"videoCompare","_key":"b164","caption":"Real Seedance 2.0 output on Melius, the same model across four different jobs. ByteDance says 2.5 extends work like this to 30-second clips with synced audio and higher resolution.","clips":[{"label":"Product","src":"/videos/blog/seedance-2-5/seedance-product.mp4","poster":"/images/hero/hero-1.webp","aspectRatio":"16 / 9"},{"label":"Character","src":"/videos/blog/seedance-2-5/seedance-character.mp4","poster":"/images/hero/hero-2.webp","aspectRatio":"16 / 9"},{"label":"Cinematic","src":"/videos/blog/seedance-2-5/seedance-cinematic.mp4","poster":"/images/hero/hero-3.webp","aspectRatio":"16 / 9"},{"label":"Motion","src":"/videos/blog/seedance-2-5/seedance-motion.mp4","poster":"/images/hero/hero-4.webp","aspectRatio":"16 / 9"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b166","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b165","text":"A quarter of every video, and climbing","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"a-quarter-of-every-video-and-climbing"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b170","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b167","text":"Seedance is not something people just experiment with and forget on the platform. It is behind ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b168","text":"26.7% of every video generation on Melius","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b169","text":", one of only two models that carry the bulk of video work here, and around 4,400 teams have used it. Last week its share sat near 30%. What makes that striking is how fast it happened.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"lineChart","_key":"b171","title":"The rise of Seedance on Melius","caption":"Seedance's share of finished video jobs on Melius. From low usage in March to more than a quarter of all video work, Seedance has become one of the most-used video models on our platform.","unit":"%","yMax":30,"points":[{"label":"March","value":1},{"label":"April","value":10},{"label":"May","value":22},{"label":"June","value":30}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b173","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b172","text":"That entire climb ran on Seedance 2.0 alone. Nobody was pushed onto it or was even the model that we automatically routed to. People tried it against everything else we offer, side by side, and kept coming back. That is the useful signal, and it is why a generational update matters more here than a version number usually would.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b178","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b175","href":"/blog/nano-banana-image-models","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b174","text":"There is a tell in how they use it. In our data, where we can see the mode, image-to-video beats text-to-video by more than a 2:1 ratio. This is the proof that Seedance is used for production quality videos, not just novelty prompting. People are not prompting Seedance with â€œcat astronautâ€ to see what happens. They are bringing a frame they already validated, a product, a character, a locked composition they love, and asking the model to move it and use it as a reference without breaking it. That starting frame is usually generated in an image model first, which is why picking the ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b176","text":"right Nano Banana model","marks":["b175"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b177","text":" for the still feeds directly into a better clip.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b180","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b179","text":"What AI video generation models struggle at","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"what-ai-video-generation-models-struggle-at"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b182","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b181","text":"A clip that looks good for three seconds is a solved problem. Most modern models clear that bar. The hard part, the part that decides whether AI video is a toy or a tool, is everything after those three seconds: holding a face across a shot, keeping a product on-model, running long enough to be usable, carrying its own sound, and not forcing you to throw away a great take because one detail came out wrong.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b184","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b183","text":"Read the Seedance 2.5 feature list with that in mind and it stops looking like a spec dump. Every headline item is aimed at exactly those production problems. One caveat before the list: Seedance 2.5 is not public yet, so what follows is ByteDance's announcement, not a benchmark.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b187","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b185","text":"Up to 50 reference inputs, from 12. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b186","text":"Four times the old ceiling, spanning images, audio, 3D white models, and style guides. That is what it takes to hold a character or a brand steady across a longer shot instead of watching it drift frame by frame.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b190","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b188","text":"Native audio, in one pass. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b189","text":"Audio and video are generated together, in the same latent space, so sound is matched to the action rather than dubbed on afterward. This is the feature people already fake today with a separate audio step.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b193","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b191","text":"Local editing. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b192","text":"Fix one region of a clip without regenerating the whole thing, so a single wrong detail no longer costs you the take you liked. At 30 seconds a clip, that is the difference between an edit and a reroll.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b196","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b194","text":"Longer clips. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b195","text":"Up to 30 seconds in a single generation, with a beta mode reaching three minutes. Long enough to be a finished shot, not a loop.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"portableTable","_key":"b197","rows":[{"_key":"b198","cells":[{"_key":"b199","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b201","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b200","text":"Capability","marks":["strong"]}]}]},{"_key":"b202","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b204","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b203","text":"Seedance 2.5 (as announced)","marks":["strong"]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b205","cells":[{"_key":"b206","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b208","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b207","text":"Reference inputs","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b209","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b211","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b210","text":"Up to 50 multimodal inputs, up from 12","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b212","cells":[{"_key":"b213","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b215","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b214","text":"Audio","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b216","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b218","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b217","text":"Co-processed with video for native sound sync","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b219","cells":[{"_key":"b220","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b222","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b221","text":"Editing","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b223","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b225","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b224","text":"Local region edits without a full regeneration","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b226","cells":[{"_key":"b227","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b229","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b228","text":"Clip length","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b230","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b232","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b231","text":"Up to 30 seconds, with a beta mode reaching 3 minutes","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b233","cells":[{"_key":"b234","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b236","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b235","text":"Resolution","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b237","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b239","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b238","text":"4K capabilities, 10-bit color (claimed, disputed)","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b240","cells":[{"_key":"b241","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b243","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b242","text":"Prompt adherence","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b244","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b246","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b245","text":"Around 20% better, per ByteDance","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b247","cells":[{"_key":"b248","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b250","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b249","text":"Availability","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b251","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b253","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b252","text":"Enterprise beta now, public access in early July 2026","marks":[]}]}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b255","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b254","text":"The references jump and the audio change are the two most consistently reported, and the two that map most directly onto how people already work. ByteDance also reports 4K capabilities with 10-bit color and roughly 20% better prompt adherence, though the 4K figure is muddier than the rest, since the same event also gave Seedance 2.0 its own native 4K tier. It was announced June 23, 2026, and is to release soon.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b257","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b256","text":"Get ready for it","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"get-ready-for-it"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b259","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b258","text":"Seedance 2.5 joins the Melius video picker the moment public access opens. Three things set you up before it does.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b262","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b260","text":"Keep building with Seedance 2.0. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b261","text":"It is one of the strongest video models on the platform today, and everything you learn about prompting it carries straight over.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b265","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b263","text":"Gather your references. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b264","text":"If you have been rationing reference frames to fit the old limit, start collecting the full set now. 2.5 gives you room for up to 50.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b271","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b268","href":"/blog/one-subscription-every-model","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b266","text":"Compare it against the field. ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b267","text":"Run the same shot through Seedance, Kling, and Veo side by side. Because Melius puts ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b269","text":"every model under one subscription","marks":["b268"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b270","text":" and one credit pool, you can mass produce and compare videos without juggling separate budgets, so you get the best chance of landing the video you want.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b273","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b272","text":"If 2.5 delivers even most of what is on the sheet, it sharpens every reason people picked it in the first place.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b279","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b276","href":"/blog/seedance-2-5","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b274","text":"Update: ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b275","text":"Seedance 2.5 has since shipped. See ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b277","text":"what Seedance 2.5 actually supports on Melius","marks":["b276"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b278","text":" for the released specs.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b284","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b281","href":"https://app.melius.com","isNewTab":true}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b280","text":"Want to see how your own generations break down by model? ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b282","text":"Open Melius","marks":["b281"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b283","text":".","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b286","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b285","text":"Frequently asked questions","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"frequently-asked-questions"},{"_type":"faqList","_key":"b287","items":"$43:props:post:faq"}]}}]
44:["$","$L42","post-nano-banana-image-models",{"post":{"_key":"post-nano-banana-image-models","slug":"nano-banana-image-models","category":"modus-news","title":"Which Nano Banana model should you use?","isoDate":"2026-07-03","date":"July 3, 2026","readTime":"8 Min Read","excerpt":"Google's Nano Banana is three image models, not one. We pulled real generation data from Melius to work out when to use Nano Banana Pro, Nano Banana 2, and Nano Banana 2 Lite.","image":{"url":"/images/blog/nano-banana/cover.webp"},"authors":[{"name":"Alex Chen, Member of Technical Staff"}],"faq":[{"question":"What is Google Nano Banana?","answer":"Nano Banana is a family of three Google image models: Nano Banana Pro (Gemini 3 Pro Image), Nano Banana 2 (Gemini 3.1 Flash Image), and Nano Banana 2 Lite (Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Image). Each one trades speed for quality a little differently."},{"question":"What is the difference between Nano Banana Pro and Nano Banana 2?","answer":"Nano Banana Pro is a reasoning model. It is slower, but it keeps branding and characters consistent, renders readable text, and outputs native 4K. Nano Banana 2 is a faster mid-tier model that leans more cinematic and creative."},{"question":"Is Nano Banana 2 better than Nano Banana Pro?","answer":"It depends on the job. Nano Banana 2 is faster and scores higher in blind text-to-image voting, so it can look better at a glance. Nano Banana Pro wins the work that matters commercially: consistency across a set, readable text, native 4K, and following a full style guide. On Melius, Pro gets used about five times as much as Nano Banana 2, so the model people vote for is not the model they run."},{"question":"Can Nano Banana Pro make videos?","answer":"No. Every Nano Banana model, including Pro, is an image model. For video you route to a dedicated video model like Kling, Veo or Seedance. On Melius you can do both on one canvas: generate the still in Nano Banana Pro, then feed it straight into a video model."},{"question":"Which Nano Banana model is fastest?","answer":"Nano Banana 2 Lite. In our data it averages about 9 to 10 seconds, roughly three to four times faster than the other two. Reach for it when you are iterating quickly."},{"question":"Which Nano Banana model is best for text in images?","answer":"Nano Banana Pro. Its reasoning step is what lets it render readable text and hold a layout together, so commercial users lean on it for anything with words in the frame."},{"question":"Does a higher benchmark score mean a model is better for my work?","answer":"Not really. A blind single-image vote rewards whatever looks best at a glance. Real work rewards other things, like consistency across a set and readable text. That is why Pro gets the most real use even though its lighter siblings score higher on text-to-image boards."}],"tableOfContents":[{"_key":"what-are-the-nano-banana-models","title":"The Models"},{"_key":"how-fast-is-each-model","title":"Speed"},{"_key":"what-do-people-actually-build-with-each","title":"What People Build"},{"_key":"how-do-they-score-on-benchmarks","title":"Benchmarks"},{"_key":"why-doesn-t-benchmark-rank-match-real-usage","title":"Rank vs Usage"},{"_key":"some-models-are-interchangeable-some-aren-t","title":"Interchangeable Models"},{"_key":"so-which-should-you-use","title":"Which to Use"},{"_key":"frequently-asked-questions","title":"FAQ"}],"content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b8","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b1","text":"Google's Nano Banana model family has been at the frontier of image generation. The three frontier Nano Banana models are all built for a different job. ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b2","text":"Nano Banana Pro","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b3","text":" is the one for polished commercial work that needs consistent branding and readable text. ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b4","text":"Nano Banana 2","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b5","text":" is faster and leans cinematic. ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b6","text":"Nano Banana 2 Lite","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b7","text":" is for cheap, quick iteration, usually under ten seconds a go.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b10","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b9","text":"At Melius we route across every major image and video model rather than betting on one. That gives us a view most people don't have. We can see which model someone picks, what they make with it, how long they wait, and how the choice changes with the job. Here is what our generation data says about the Nano Banana family, and where the real usage parts ways with the leaderboards.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b12","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b11","text":"What are the Nano Banana models?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"what-are-the-nano-banana-models"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b14","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b13","text":"There are three models in the family, and each one trades speed for quality differently.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b17","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b15","text":"Nano Banana Pro (Gemini 3 Pro Image) ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b16","text":"is a reasoning model. It works through the image before rendering, so it handles complex compositions, keeps text readable, and holds branding and characters steady across a shot. That thinking time makes it the slowest of the three.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b20","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b18","text":"Nano Banana 2 (Gemini 3.1 Flash Image) ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b19","text":"is the middle option. It's quick and flexible, and it covers most creative work well.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b23","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b21","text":"Nano Banana 2 Lite (Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Image) ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b22","text":"is built for speed. Use it for cheap, throwaway iterations, not final work.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"portableTable","_key":"b24","rows":[{"_key":"b25","cells":[{"_key":"b26","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b28","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b27","text":"Model","marks":["strong"]}]}]},{"_key":"b29","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b31","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b30","text":"Best for","marks":["strong"]}]}]},{"_key":"b32","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b34","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b33","text":"Relative speed","marks":["strong"]}]}]},{"_key":"b35","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b37","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b36","text":"Signature use","marks":["strong"]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b38","cells":[{"_key":"b39","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b41","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b40","text":"Nano Banana Pro","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b42","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b44","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b43","text":"Polish & brand fidelity","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b45","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b47","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b46","text":"Slowest","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b48","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b50","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b49","text":"Campaigns, product & food shots, in-image text","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b51","cells":[{"_key":"b52","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b54","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b53","text":"Nano Banana 2","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b55","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b57","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b56","text":"Creative exploration","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b58","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b60","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b59","text":"Fast","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b61","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b63","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b62","text":"Cinematic frames, concept art, characters","marks":[]}]}]}]},{"_key":"b64","cells":[{"_key":"b65","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b67","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b66","text":"Nano Banana 2 Lite","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b68","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b70","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b69","text":"Quick iteration","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b71","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b73","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b72","text":"Fastest","marks":[]}]}]},{"_key":"b74","text":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b76","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b75","text":"Rapid prototyping tests","marks":[]}]}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b78","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b77","text":"How fast is each model?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"how-fast-is-each-model"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b80","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b79","text":"The speed differences track the lineup almost exactly.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"barChart","_key":"b81","title":"Generation latency by model (seconds)","caption":"End-to-end wall-clock time across finished jobs on Melius. Lower is faster.","unit":"s","baseline":0,"series":["Average","Median"],"categories":[{"label":"Nano Banana 2 Lite","values":[9.7,9]},{"label":"Nano Banana 2","values":[29.7,21.5]},{"label":"Nano Banana Pro","values":[38,28.8]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b83","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b82","text":"Lite is much faster, roughly three to four times quicker than the others. Pro is the slowest on average, which is what you'd expect from a reasoning model. Look at the medians, though, and Pro (28.8s) and Nano Banana 2 (21.5s) aren't far apart. The difference is in the tail. Pro has more very slow jobs, and those tend to be the complex compositions where it spends longer working through the image.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b85","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b84","text":"Two caveats. These are end-to-end times that include any queue wait, not pure model inference. And Lite's sample is small, only 238 jobs, so treat its numbers as rough.","marks":["em"]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b87","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b86","text":"What do people actually build with each?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"what-do-people-actually-build-with-each"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b89","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b88","text":"Latency tells you what a model can do quickly but the prompts tell you what people actually reach for it to do, and here the three variants split apart more than we expected.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b92","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b90","text":"Nano Banana Pro ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b91","text":"handles the commercial work. Its prompts read like production briefs: long, style-locked instructions for campaigns, ads, fashion editorials, and product shots. Food and drink photography shows up far more here than on any other model, around 12.6% of Pro's prompts against 5% or less elsewhere.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b95","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b93","text":"Nano Banana 2 ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b94","text":"leans creative. It gets nearly double the cinematic, film-style prompts that Pro does, about 19.5% against 9.7%, with people calling out lenses, camera angles, and mood, or building out characters and worlds.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b98","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b96","text":"Nano Banana 2 Lite ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b97","text":"gets the least use, mostly quick portrait and landscape tests. It's the pass people make before committing a real job to Pro.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"bullet","level":1},{"_type":"imageCompare","_key":"b99","caption":"The same prompt run through all three models, a travel flat-lay: â€œa passport, film camera, sunglasses, coins and a boarding pass reading â€˜JFK to LHRâ€™, â€˜GATE 22â€™, seat â€˜14Aâ€™ arranged on a wooden table, top-down, natural light.â€ Look at the composition and the small text on the boarding pass. Pro keeps the layout tidy and the type readable. Nano Banana 2 stays close. Lite gets looser, with softer text and a messier arrangement.","images":[{"label":"Nano Banana Pro","src":"/images/blog/nano-banana/pro.jpg","alt":"Travel flat-lay generated by Nano Banana Pro: passport, film camera, boarding pass reading JFK to LHR, GATE 22, seat 14A, sunglasses and coins on a wooden table","aspectRatio":"3 / 2"},{"label":"Nano Banana 2","src":"/images/blog/nano-banana/nb2.jpg","alt":"Travel flat-lay generated by Nano Banana 2: passport, Fujifilm camera, boarding pass, compass and luggage tag on a wooden table","aspectRatio":"3 / 2"},{"label":"Nano Banana 2 Lite","src":"/images/blog/nano-banana/lite.png","alt":"Travel flat-lay generated by Nano Banana 2 Lite: passport, camera, phone, boarding pass and sunglasses on a wooden table","aspectRatio":"3 / 2"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b101","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b100","text":"How do they score on benchmarks?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"how-do-they-score-on-benchmarks"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b103","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b102","text":"Our data shows how people use these models. Public benchmarks show how the general public perceives the quality of these models in blind head-to-head voting. On text-to-image, the order inside the family is a bit surprising.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"barChart","_key":"b104","title":"Blind text-to-image preference (Elo)","caption":"Higher means more preferred in blind head-to-head voting. The axis starts at 1200 so the gaps are easier to see. These boards move constantly, so read it as a snapshot.","unit":"","baseline":1200,"series":["Text-to-image Elo"],"categories":[{"label":"Nano Banana 2","values":[1270]},{"label":"Nano Banana 2 Lite","values":[1251]},{"label":"Nano Banana Pro","values":[1238]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b106","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b105","text":"Editing flips the order. On single-image edits Pro moves to the front of the family, around 1385 to 1388, ahead of Nano Banana 2, with Lite lower at about 1308. That fits. Editing means reading and changing an existing image, which is where Pro's reasoning and consistency actually pay off.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b108","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b107","text":"Why doesn't benchmark rank match real usage?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"why-doesn-t-benchmark-rank-match-real-usage"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b110","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b109","text":"The interesting part is the mismatch. In blind text-to-image voting, both Nano Banana 2 and even Lite, the cheapest and fastest model, score higher than Pro. On Melius, though, Pro gets used about five times as much as Nano Banana 2. The model people prefer in a vote isn't the model they run.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b112","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b111","text":"A blind vote rewards whatever image looks best on its own. Real work asks for more than that since consistency is key across a whole campaign, text you can read, 4K output, and a model that can take a full style guide as reference and follow it. That's what Nano Banana Pro is built for, and it's the same place the editing benchmarks put it ahead.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b114","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b113","text":"Some models are interchangeable, some aren't","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"some-models-are-interchangeable-some-aren-t"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b116","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b115","text":"We compared Nano Banana Pro with another leading commercial model, and the usage looked almost identical. Both sat around 56% product and e-commerce work and about 16% portraits. That isn't a coincidence. People treat the two as interchangeable and run the same prompt through both to compare. For commercial users they're two tools that do the same job, which is the whole argument for routing across providers.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b118","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b117","text":"Other models look nothing like this. Nano Banana is mostly commercial work, but some models lean toward portraits and people (around 30%) and cinematic or video work (around 25%). They also carry a small adult tail, about 2.6%, that barely registers on Nano Banana, and their prompts run far more international. Same platform, very different crowds.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b120","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b119","text":"So which should you use?","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"so-which-should-you-use"},{"_type":"block","_key":"b122","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b121","text":"Put simply:","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b125","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b123","text":"Brand campaign, product shots, or anything with text in the frame? ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b124","text":"Use Nano Banana Pro, and try the same prompt on another commercial model too. Your peers already do.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b128","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b126","text":"Selling food or drink? ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b127","text":"Pro again. It does unusually well on this.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b131","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b129","text":"After a cinematic frame, concept art, or a character sheet? ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b130","text":"Nano Banana 2, and it comes back faster.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b134","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b132","text":"Just testing an idea? ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b133","text":"Nano Banana 2 Lite, usually under ten seconds a go.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b137","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b135","text":"Making entertainment or consumer content for a global audience? ","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b136","text":"Some of the other models on the platform fit that better.","marks":[]}],"listItem":"number","level":1},{"_type":"block","_key":"b145","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b139","href":"/blog/one-subscription-every-model","isNewTab":false},{"_type":"link","_key":"b142","href":"/blog/seedance-2-5-anticipation","isNewTab":false}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b138","text":"None of these is the best image model in the abstract. There's just the model that fits the shot in front of you. That's why Melius runs ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b140","text":"every model under one subscription","marks":["b139"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b141","text":", so you can pick per job instead of committing to one. When the shot needs to move, the same logic carries over to video, where ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b143","text":"Seedance","marks":["b142"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b144","text":" is one of the most-used models on the platform.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b150","style":"normal","markDefs":[{"_type":"link","_key":"b147","href":"https://app.melius.com","isNewTab":true}],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b146","text":"Want to see how your own generations break down by model? ","marks":[]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b148","text":"Open Melius","marks":["b147"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"b149","text":".","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b152","style":"h2","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"b151","text":"Frequently asked questions","marks":[]}],"anchorId":"frequently-asked-questions"},{"_type":"faqList","_key":"b153","items":"$44:props:post:faq"}]}}]
